, "places": [{"name": "Los Angeles, United States", "role": "Cultural Hub"}, {"name": "New York City, United States", "role": "Major Influence"}, {"name": "San Francisco, United States", "role": "Birthplace"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: funk metal", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "funk-metal"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q667659", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Funk_metal"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "funk metal contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Funk metal significantly impacted the music scene in the late 1980s and 1990s, influencing future genres and encouraging artists to address social issues, particularly race and identity, in their work.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-30T06:02:46Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28423575421", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Subgenre of funk rock and alternative metal Created c. 1985.", "newValue": "Funk metal emerged in the mid-1980s, primarily within the United States, as a distinct subgenre blending elements of funk rock and alternative metal. Artists and bands such as Red Hot Chili Peppers, Faith No More, and Living Colour played pivotal roles in shaping this genre, which combined intricate rhythms and grooves with the aggressive tones of metal. Its fusion of styles created a significant cultural movement that not only impacted the music industry but also resonated with the socio-political climate of the time, addressing themes of race, identity, and community.

The genre's rise can be traced back to the release of albums like the Red Hot Chili Peppers' "The Uplift Mofo Party Plan" (1987) and Faith No More's "The Real Thing" (1989), which showcased innovative guitar work and dynamic vocal styles that captivated audiences. The 1990s saw funk metal gain mainstream popularity, with the genre producing notable singles that charted on Billboard, such as Living Colour's "Cult of Personality". This period also coincided with a growing interest in alternative music, influenced by the punk and funk movements of previous decades, and culminated in significant performances and festival appearances that solidified its place in rock history.

